{
  "requirement": "When implementation shows end-user-observable behavior no requirement covers, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to draft a requirement for it and take it through review, agreement, and commit before implementing that behavior.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The only clause is: \"the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to draft a requirement for it and take it through review, agreement, and commit before implementing that behavior.\" It does not prescribe a library, algorithm, signature, type, field, or delegation through a named interface.\n\nThe skill is `habeas-requirements`, and that name is only the identity of the subject. It is not a build constraint. The whole skill file is `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, and `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.text_change.uncovered` has an implements edge to it.\n\nThe obligation is about what the skill's instructions say: draft a requirement, review it, get agreement, commit it, and only then implement. Each ordering and content point is a statement about the text's observable content. A different arrangement of the same instructions would preserve the behavior. For example, the guidance could sit in another section, such as `section[Workflow]` or `section[Gotchas]`, or be worded differently. The text would still satisfy the requirement as long as it gives the direction. Where the guidance sits and how it is phrased are therefore discretionary.\n\nNo further implementation root is needed. Whether the guidance is present and correct is a behavioral or content question for the behavioral criterion, not a structural gap.\n\nbehavioral: **Obligation.** The requirement has one clause. When implementation shows end-user-observable behavior that no requirement covers, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to do four things. They draft a requirement for it, review it, get agreement on it, and commit it. All of this happens before they implement that behavior. All of these are properties of the skill text in `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`.\n\n**What the skill says.**\n- The Workflow paragraph says: \"Behavior no requirement covers goes back to step 2.\"\n- Step 2 is \"Draft.\", step 3 is \"Review.\", and step 4 is \"Present each group to the user…\". Step 4 also carries the **Gate:** \"no implementation or test code, and no commit, until the user has agreed to, rejected, or deferred every group\".\n- Step 5 is \"Commit… all groups together, before any code\".\n- Step 6 is \"Implement and test\".\n\n**Test mapping.**\n- `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_drafts_a_requirement_for_uncovered_behavior]` asserts the redirect sentence through `assert_phrases`. That covers the trigger and the direction to go back to drafting. It asserts that step 2 starts with \"Draft.\". That covers drafting. It asserts that step 3 starts with \"Review.\" and that step 4 starts with \"Present each group to the user\". That covers the review and agreement stages the redirect passes through. A skill that redirected elsewhere, or that renumbered or dropped these steps, would fail.\n- `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_commits_every_group_together_before_code]` asserts that the commit step contains \"then commit it, all groups together, before any code\". It asserts that the commit step comes after the step containing **Gate:**. That covers commit, and ordering of the agreement gate before commit and code. Together with the \"before any code\" wording, this covers \"before implementing that behavior\".\n\n**Assessment.** Each clause (draft, review, agreement, commit, all before implementation) maps to an assertion that checks the skill text. The helpers `step`, `step_of` and `assert_phrases` are reached from the marked tests. They really fail on a missing step or phrase, through `panic!` or `assert_text`. Both tests carry `{test}` markers for the subject. I found no unmarked relevant test. The \"end-user-observable\" qualifier is only the trigger description. The skill's step 1 already scopes drafting to observable behavior. The text does not call for a separate assertion on it, so I see no gap.",
